        <body>
            <div xml:id="d625">

                <pb facs="1011" n="875" xml:id="pg875"/>

                <head>No. 625.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Merrill to Mr. Bayard.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Honolulu</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1888-10-06"><hi rendition="#italic">October</hi> 6, 1888</date>.
                        (Received November 3.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 213.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that H. B.
                    M. S. <hi rendition="#italic">Hyacinth</hi>, Captain Bourke commanding, left
                    this harbor on the 3d instant on a cruise in the south.</p>
                <p>While the impression prevails here that the captain has departed for Samoa, yet,
                    in fact, I am reliably informed, he goes direct to Raratonga, one of the group
                    of “Cook’s Islands”. or “Harvey Archipelago,” situated about 20 degrees south
                    latitude, and 157 degrees west longitude.</p>
                <p>The cruise is for the purpose of taking possession of, or establishing a
                    protectorate over, the group on behalf of Great Britain.</p>
                <p>This action is alleged to be in response to a request of the authorities at
                    Raratonga, who, it is claimed, fear occupation by the French.</p>
                <p>I have, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Geo. W. Merrill.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
